Rem Capability: net
Rem ==================================================================
Rem seedhttps.shoddy — reckoner seed: https.shoddy, a reply taken apart
Rem
Rem seednet fetches a URL and hands back a wall of text. This seed is
Rem the half that makes the wall useful: HTTPSTATUS, HTTPHEADER,
Rem HTTPHEADERS, HTTPREASON and HTTPBODY take a reply apart, and
Rem HTTPSGET fetches one with headers of your own choosing.
Rem
Rem THE EASIEST SEED IN THE DIRECTORY, and https.shoddy earned that. It
Rem has NOT ONE Error in it, and its header states the rule the rest of
Rem this file leans on: "PURE WHERE IT CAN BE... Building a request and
Rem parsing a reply are functions on Strings... Only HttpsGet and
Rem HttpsRequest reach the network."
Rem
Rem So five of the six words below are the machine's own, bridged
Rem straight through with nothing but an argument check. The sixth
Rem replaces HttpsRequest, and only because that one goes through
Rem net.shoddy's RequestTls and the ABORTING socket builtins under it.
Rem The request TEXT is still built by HttpRequestText — the machine's
Rem own, byte for byte — and only the sending is done differently.
Rem
Rem NETBODY IS GONE, AND THIS IS WHERE IT WENT. seednet carried a
Rem NETBODY that split a reply at the blank line, written before this
Rem seed existed. HTTPBODY is https.shoddy's own and does the same job
Rem properly, so keeping both would have been the second spelling of a
Rem word the dictionary already had — the thing seedbuiltin's own rules
Rem exist to prevent. A mill that wants replies parsed folds this seed.
Rem
Rem HEADERS ARE A DICT, in and out, which is seeddict's shape and
Rem dict.shoddy's association list: { ("user-agent" "shoddy") }. Names
Rem come back LOWERCASED because HTTP header names are case-insensitive
Rem and https.shoddy normalises at the boundary, so DGET finds
Rem content-type whichever way the server wrote it.
Rem
Rem WHAT IS NOT BRIDGED, and it is the machine's own limit rather than
Rem this seed's: HTTP/1.0, no redirects followed, no compression. A 301
Rem is REPORTED — HTTPSTATUS says 301 and HTTPHEADER finds the location
Rem — because chasing one means deciding about loops, cross-host hops
Rem and method rewriting, which https.shoddy calls the caller's policy
Rem rather than a library's. A session is a caller like any other.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "https.shoddy"
Include "cuttle.shoddy"
Include "reckoner.shoddy"
Include "seq.shoddy"

Rem ---- headers, across the bridge ---------------------------------------

Rem A dict cell to the List Of Pair https.shoddy wants. An empty list is
Rem the ordinary case — most requests need no headers of their own — so
Rem it is not an error, and a caller who wants none types { }.
Def HsHeadersOf(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case RckListArg(word, c)
        Case Ok(xs)
            HsHeadersAt(word, xs, { })
        Case Err(w, at)
            Err(w, at)
        Case Else
            Err(word & " did not answer", 0)

Def HsHeadersAt(word As String, xs As List Of Cell, acc As List Of Pair) As Result
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        Ok(acc)
    Else
        HsHeadersNext(word, xs, acc, HsPairOf(word, First(xs)))

Def HsHeadersNext(word As String, xs As List Of Cell, acc As List Of Pair, r As Result) As Result
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(p)
            HsHeadersAt(word, Rest(xs), Append(acc, p))
        Case Err(w, at)
            Err(w, at)
        Case Else
            Err(word & " did not answer", 0)

Rem A header is a pair of STRINGs. A two-element LIST is accepted as
Rem well as a PAIR, because { "accept" "text/html" } is what somebody
Rem types before they have met the pair syntax and it is unambiguous.
Def HsPairOf(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case c
        Case CutPair(p)
            HsPairParts(word, Fst(p), Snd(p))
        Case CutList(xs)
            HsPairList(word, xs)
        Case Else
            Err(word & ": A HEADER IS A NAME AND A VALUE, LIKE ( \"accept\" \"text/html\" )", 0)

Def HsPairList(word As String, xs As List Of Cell) As Result
    If Length(xs) <> 2 Then
        Err(word & ": A HEADER IS A NAME AND A VALUE, AND THAT HAS " & Str(Length(xs)) & " PARTS", 0)
    Else
        HsPairParts(word, First(xs), Nth(xs, 2))

Def HsPairParts(word As String, a As Cell, b As Cell) As Result
    Select Case Pair(a, b)
        Case Pair(CutStr(n), CutStr(v))
            HsPairNamed(word, n, v)
        Case Else
            Err(word & ": A HEADER'S NAME AND VALUE MUST BOTH BE STRINGS", 0)

Rem A newline inside a header is how a request gets split in two and a
Rem second one smuggled in behind it. Nothing downstream would notice,
Rem which is exactly why it is refused here.
Def HsPairNamed(word As String, n As String, v As String) As Result
    If HsHasBreak(n) Or HsHasBreak(v) Then
        Err(word & ": A HEADER CANNOT CONTAIN A LINE BREAK — THAT WOULD BE TWO REQUESTS", 0)
    Else
        HsPairMade(word, n, v)

Def HsHasBreak(s As String) As Boolean
    (Instr(s, "\r") > 0) Or (Instr(s, "\n") > 0)

Def HsPairMade(word As String, n As String, v As String) As Result
    If n = "" Then
        Err(word & ": A HEADER NEEDS A NAME", 0)
    Else
        Ok(Pair(n, v))

Rem The other direction: https.shoddy's parsed headers as a dict cell.
Def HsDictCell(hs As List Of Pair) As Cell
    CutList(Map(hs, HsEntry))

Def HsEntry(p As Pair) As Cell
    CutPair(Pair(CutStr(Fst(p)), CutStr(Snd(p))))

Rem ---- the pure half, bridged straight through ---------------------------

Def HsSeedRead(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = RckReg(st, "HTTPSTATUS", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => HsStatus(vs)), "( reply -- n )", "The status number of an HTTP reply — 200, 404, 301 — and 0 for something that is not a reply at all.")
    Let s2 = RckReg(s1, "HTTPREASON", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => HsReason(vs)), "( reply -- str )", "The reason phrase beside the status: \"OK\", \"Not Found\".")
    Let s3 = RckReg(s2, "HTTPBODY", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => HsBody(vs)), "( reply -- str )", "Everything after the blank line that ends the headers. Bytes as they arrived, one per character.")
    Let s4 = RckReg(s3, "HTTPHEADERS", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => HsHeaders(vs)), "( reply -- dict )", "Every header as a dict, names lowercased. DGET reads one out.")
    RckReg(s4, "HTTPHEADER", 3, RckPure(Fn(vs) => HsHeader(vs)), "( reply name dflt -- str )", "One header by name, or dflt when it is not there. The name is matched without regard to case.")

Def HsStatus(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    HsOnReply("HTTPSTATUS", vs, Fn(r) => CutNum(HttpStatus(r)))

Def HsReason(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    HsOnReply("HTTPREASON", vs, Fn(r) => CutStr(HttpReason(r)))

Def HsBody(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    HsOnReply("HTTPBODY", vs, Fn(r) => CutStr(HttpBody(r)))

Def HsHeaders(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    HsOnReply("HTTPHEADERS", vs, Fn(r) => HsDictCell(HttpHeaders(r)))

Rem Every one of the four takes a reply and nothing else, and none of
Rem them can fail on its contents: https.shoddy answers 0, "" or an
Rem empty dict for text that is not a reply, which is the right answer
Rem for a caller holding something unexpected.
Def HsOnReply(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ String -- Cell ]) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg(word, First(vs))
        Case Ok(reply)
            RckVals({ Call(f, reply) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def HsHeader(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckTextArg("HTTPHEADER", Nth(vs, 1)), RckTextArg("HTTPHEADER", Nth(vs, 2)))
        Case Pair(Ok(reply), Ok(name))
            HsHeaderDflt(reply, name, Nth(vs, 3))
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("HTTPHEADER needs a reply and a header name")

Def HsHeaderDflt(reply As String, name As String, dc As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg("HTTPHEADER", dc)
        Case Ok(dflt)
            RckVals({ CutStr(HttpHeader(reply, name, dflt)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("HTTPHEADER did not answer")

Rem ---- the one word that reaches the network -----------------------------

Def HsSeedGet(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = RckReg(st, "HTTPSGET", 3, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => HsGet(vs)), "( host path headers -- reply )", "Fetch a path from a host over TLS with headers of your own, and answer the whole raw reply. Headers are a dict: { ( \"user-agent\" \"shoddy\" ) }. Use { } for none — though a good many hosts answer 403 to a request without a user-agent.")
    RckReg(s1, "HTTPSREQUEST", 4, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => HsRequest(vs)), "( method host path headers -- reply )", "The same for any method — GET, HEAD, DELETE. No body is sent, which is what HTTP/1.0 makes straightforward.")

Def HsGet(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    HsFetch("HTTPSGET", "GET", Nth(vs, 1), Nth(vs, 2), Nth(vs, 3))

Def HsRequest(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg("HTTPSREQUEST", Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(method)
            HsRequestMethod("HTTPSREQUEST", method, vs)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("HTTPSREQUEST did not answer")

Rem A method with a space or a break in it would put a second line into
Rem the request, the same fault a header break would.
Def HsRequestMethod(word As String, method As String, vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    If method = "" Or HsHasBreak(method) Or Instr(method, " ") > 0 Then
        RckWorkBad(word & ": A METHOD IS ONE WORD, LIKE \"GET\" OR \"HEAD\"")
    Else
        HsFetch(word, method, Nth(vs, 2), Nth(vs, 3), Nth(vs, 4))

Def HsFetch(word As String, method As String, hc As Cell, pc As Cell, hdc As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckTextArg(word, hc), RckTextArg(word, pc))
        Case Pair(Ok(host), Ok(path))
            HsFetchHeaders(word, method, host, path, HsHeadersOf(word, hdc))
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " needs a host and a path")

Def HsFetchHeaders(word As String, method As String, host As String, path As String, r As Result) As RckWork
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(hs)
            HsFetchSound(word, method, host, path, hs)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Rem A path must start with a slash or the request line is malformed, and
Rem a host with a break in it is the same smuggling fault as a header's.
Def HsFetchSound(word As String, method As String, host As String, path As String, hs As List Of Pair) As RckWork
    If host = "" Or HsHasBreak(host) Then
        RckWorkBad(word & ": THAT IS NOT A HOST NAME")
    Else
        HsFetchPathed(word, method, host, path, hs)

Def HsFetchPathed(word As String, method As String, host As String, path As String, hs As List Of Pair) As RckWork
    If HsHasBreak(path) Or Instr(path, " ") > 0 Then
        RckWorkBad(word & ": A PATH CANNOT CONTAIN A SPACE OR A LINE BREAK — ESCAPE THEM AS %20")
    Else
        HsFetchAt(word, method, host, HsPathOf(path), hs)

Rem A bare "" or "index.html" is what somebody types before they think
Rem about it, and both mean the same thing with a slash on the front.
Def HsPathOf(path As String) As String
    If path = "" Then
        "/"
    Else
        If Left(path, 1) = "/" Then
            path
        Else
            "/" & path

Rem THE ONE PLACE THIS SEED DIFFERS FROM THE MACHINE. HttpsRequest sends
Rem through net.shoddy's RequestTls, which is built on the aborting
Rem socket builtins; TRYTCPREQUEST does the whole conversation and
Rem reports. The request TEXT is still HttpRequestText's, byte for byte,
Rem so what goes on the wire is what the machine would have sent.
Def HsFetchAt(word As String, method As String, host As String, path As String, hs As List Of Pair) As RckWork
    If Not NetAllowed() Then
        RckWorkBad(word & ": THE NETWORK IS OFF — START THE MILL WITH --allow-net")
    Else
        HsAnswered(word, host, TryTcpRequest(host, HttpsPort(), True,
                                             HttpRequestText(method, path, host, hs)))

Def HsAnswered(word As String, host As String, r As Result) As RckWork
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(reply)
            RckVals({ CutStr(reply) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(word & ": " & w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Rem ---- the seed ------------------------------------------------------------

Def RckSeedHttps(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = HsSeedRead(RckSeeding(st, "seed-https"))
    HsSeedGet(s1)
